---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { content } from '../data/content';

const t = content.en;
const github = 'https://github.com/hackills/munadi-apks';
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  title="Privacy — GitHub build — Munadi"
  description="How the GitHub build of Munadi handles data: no accounts, ads, analytics, or trackers. Approximate location is used only for qibla, the place name, and the prayer-time area."
>
  <Header lang="en" t={t} home="/" alternatePath="/ar/privacy/" />

  <main id="main">
    <section class="document-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
        <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
        <p>
          Munadi is a simple, offline prayer-times app that began in Qatar.
          This policy covers the <strong>GitHub build</strong> of Munadi, the signed APK
          downloaded from GitHub Releases. That build can check GitHub for updates and
          install them itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="container">
      <article class="document">
        <p class="effective">Effective: 22 November 2026</p>

        <p class="note">
          <strong>Installed from Google Play?</strong> The Play build uses the internet
          only for the optional mosque map and has no in-app updater, so it has its own policy:
          <a href="/privacy/play/">read the Google Play build policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Summary</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>No accounts, ads, analytics, or trackers.</li>
          <li>Prayer times and settings stay on your device.</li>
          <li>
            Approximate location is used for the qibla compass, the place name, and the
            prayer-time area.
          </li>
          <li>
            Precise location is asked for only by the Qatar mosque finder, and stays on
            your device.
          </li>
          <li>We do not operate a server that receives your data.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Data the app uses</h2>

        <h3>Approximate location</h3>
        <p>
          Approximate location is used to calculate the qibla direction and distance,
          show a place name, and suggest the nearest prayer-time area. These features
          never ask for precise location. The last approximate location and place are
          cached in the app’s private storage so the qibla screen can open quickly.
        </p>
        <p>
          Place names are matched on the device from a built-in list. When location is
          already allowed, the app may check it at launch to suggest an area. Your
          location is not sent anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          On first launch, Munadi also reads which country your mobile network and SIM
          card belong to, without any permission, only to open the right country in the
          city list. This stays on your device. Location may suggest the nearest listed
          city in any region, and never switches it for you.
        </p>

        <h3>Precise location and the mosque finder (Qatar)</h3>
        <p>
          When Qatar is the selected country, Munadi offers a mosque finder. It lists
          mosques from a directory stored inside the app and sorts them by distance from
          you. Approximate location is blurred by a kilometre or more, too much to tell
          which mosque is nearest, so this screen, and only this screen, asks for
          precise location. You can choose “Approximate” in the system dialog, or
          decline, and still browse and search the directory.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your position is read only while the mosque finder is open, is compared with
          the directory on your device, and is not stored by the finder or sent
          anywhere. When you tap Navigate or Share, Munadi hands the mosque’s
          coordinates, never yours, to the maps app or the app you choose; what that
          app does with them is covered by its own policy.
        </p>

        <h3>Mosque map (Qatar)</h3>
        <p>
          The mosque finder can also show a street map. The map is optional and asks
          before it is first used. It is drawn inside the app from map tiles requested
          from <a href="https://openfreemap.org" rel="noopener">OpenFreeMap</a>, which
          may deliver them through Cloudflare. Like any website you open, that service
          receives your device’s IP address and the map area being requested, so it can
          tell roughly which area you are looking at. Munadi does not send your
          location, any identifier, or anything about you with those requests, and
          nothing is sent to us. Tiles you have viewed may be kept in the app’s cache
          on your device. If you never open the map, none of this happens.
        </p>

        <h3>Settings and timetable</h3>
        <p>
          Theme, color, language, prayer-time city, Asr choice, Hijri date adjustment,
          iqamah adjustments, notification preferences, and sound choices are stored
          locally on your device.
        </p>
        <p>
          In Qatar, prayer times follow the Qatar Calendar House timetable, stored inside
          the app, using the Doha table by default. You can choose Abu Samra, Dukhan, or
          Al Shamal in Settings, and the app may suggest one when location is available.
        </p>
        <p>
          In other regions (currently Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait, Oman, Egypt, Morocco, Algeria, Pakistan, Türkiye, Indonesia, Singapore, and Tatarstan), prayer times are calculated on your
          device from the coordinates of the city you choose in Settings, using an
          open-source calculation library. Nothing about the calculation leaves your
          device.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can export your settings to a file you choose and import it later. The
          file is saved where you put it; Munadi does not upload it.
        </p>

        <h3>Diagnostics</h3>
        <p>
          About → Share diagnostics builds a text report for when an alert, the
          widget or the qibla compass misbehaves: the app and Android versions,
          the phone model and its compass sensors, the chosen city (by name,
          never coordinates), your alert, Do Not Disturb and display settings,
          permission and sound states, the alarms the app expects next, the
          travel check’s state (the time-zone offsets of the phone and of the
          city), how the qibla screen was last used (compass accuracy and the
          age and accuracy of the location fix, never the position or the
          direction), recent events such as restarts, travel prompts and
          settings restores, recent reasons the app was closed, and Munadi’s own
          recent log lines. It is created only when you tap the button and goes
          only where you send it from the share sheet. Munadi does not send it
          anywhere by itself.
        </p>

        <h3>Network access</h3>
        <p>
          If update checks are enabled, the app requests the latest release information
          from GitHub. GitHub receives standard network information such as your IP
          address and the app’s user agent, subject to GitHub’s privacy policy. Munadi
          does not send analytics, identifiers, or usage data.
        </p>

        <h3>Permissions</h3>
        <p>Depending on the features you use, the app may request:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Approximate location, for qibla and the prayer-time area</li>
          <li>Precise location, only for the Qatar mosque finder</li>
          <li>Notifications, for adhan and update alerts</li>
          <li>Exact alarms, to keep prayer reminders on time</li>
          <li>Battery optimisation exemption, so alarms are not delayed</li>
          <li>Internet, for optional update checks and the optional mosque map</li>
          <li>Install unknown apps, for in-app updates</li>
          <li>Foreground media playback and vibration, for the adhan</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can decline optional permissions and still use the prayer timetable.</p>

        <h2>Sharing and retention</h2>
        <p>
          We do not sell or share your data. Munadi has no accounts and no analytics
          SDK. Local data remains until you clear the app’s storage or uninstall it.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          Munadi is a general-audience prayer app and does not knowingly collect
          personal information from children.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          This policy may be updated in the public repository. The effective date above
          will change when it does.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href="mailto:munadi.prayer@gmail.com">munadi.prayer@gmail.com</a>,
          or open an issue at
          <a href={`${github}/issues`} rel="noopener">{github}/issues</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="note">
          <h3>This website</h3>
          <p>
            This site is a static page hosted on Vercel. It uses no cookies, analytics,
            or tracking scripts. Vercel processes standard request information — such
            as your IP address and user agent — to serve and protect the site, subject
            to Vercel’s privacy policy.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer lang="en" t={t} home="/" />
</Base>
